package Lessons

use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

class SceneItems2 is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("experiment/Scenes/items.html")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews. An experiment, so it stays
        // out of search: the real lesson is the one to find.
        SetDescription("Learn about cameras, and how to add, select and remove items in 2D and 3D scenes in Quorum Studio.")
        SetSearchable(false)

        Title("Add and Remove Items in a Scene")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("One of the most common things we do in a scene editor is add and remove items. The
basic idea is simple: we choose an item in the Palette, on the left, and place it in the scene editor, on the
right, with the mouse or the keyboard. We can also move the camera so we can see exactly where things go. On
this page we start with cameras, which decide what we see, and then add and remove items, first in a 2D scene
and then in a 3D scene.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Understand what a camera is in computer graphics, and how orthographic and perspective cameras differ")
        ListItem("Add items to a 2D and a 3D scene from the Palette, with the keyboard or the mouse")
        ListItem("Select an item, including one of several items in the same spot")
        ListItem("Delete an item, and undo a change")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Think of a board game you know, like chess or checkers. If you had to tell someone
over the phone where to put every piece at the start, what would you say? How would you say where a piece goes,
and what would you do if two pieces were on the same square?")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/scenes/AddRemoveItems/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_GAME_DEVELOPMENT_PROFESSIONAL_PRACTICE_9)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_SOFTWARE_DEVELOPMENT_DESIGN_AND_DEVELOPMENT_3)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_X_PLUS_COMPUTER_SCIENCE_X_PLUS_COMPUTER_SCIENCE_1)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        Cameras()
        Orthographic()
        Perspective()
        Scene2D()
        Scene3D()
        Selecting()
        Removing()
    end

    private action Cameras
        Subsection("What is a camera?")
        Paragraph("Before we add anything, it helps to know how a game decides what to show. A scene can be
much bigger than your screen, and it can have things all around it, in front, behind, above and below. The game
needs a way to pick which part to show. That's the job of the "):Term("camera"):Text(".")
        Paragraph("A camera in a game is not a real camera. It's more like a pair of eyes, or a window
you look through. It has a place where it is, and a direction it faces. Whatever is in front of it, and not too
far away, shows up on the screen. Anything outside its view, or behind it, doesn't show up at all, even though
it's still in the scene. If you want the player to see something, the camera has to be pointed at it.")
        Paragraph("Every layer in a Quorum scene has its own camera. The editor also has its own "):Term("editor camera"):Text(", which you move around while you build. The game's camera can be somewhere else
entirely, so what you see while building isn't always what the player sees.")
        Paragraph("There are two kinds of camera: orthographic and perspective.")
    end

    private action Orthographic
        Subsection("Orthographic cameras")
        Paragraph("An "):Term("orthographic"):Text(" camera shows everything at the same size, no matter how near or far it is. Imagine
standing on a chair and looking straight down at a board game on the floor. Every square on the board looks the
same size, and the pieces don't get bigger or smaller as they move around. Maps, building plans and board games
all show the world this way.")
        Paragraph("Because nothing shrinks with distance, an orthographic camera is a good fit for
flat, 2D games, where you want every tile and character to look the same everywhere on the screen. In Quorum,
every 2D layer uses an orthographic camera. In the editor, the grey outline you'll see in a 2D scene shows
exactly what this camera can see.")
    end

    private action Perspective
        Subsection("Perspective cameras")
        Paragraph("A "):Term("perspective"):Text(" camera works the way eyes do. Things close to it look big, and things far away look
small. Think of standing between railroad tracks: the rails seem to get closer together and meet far away, even
though they never really do. A tree right in front of you can look taller than a mountain in the distance.")
        Paragraph("This is what makes a 3D world feel deep and real, so 3D games use a perspective camera. In
Quorum, every 3D layer uses one. A perspective camera also has a "):Term("field of view"):Text(": how wide it can see, like the difference between looking through a narrow paper tube and
looking with your whole eyes open. A wider field of view shows more of the world at once, but everything in it
looks smaller.")
    end

    private action Scene2D
        Subsection("Changing the 2D Scene")
        Paragraph("The first step to adding an item is selecting the Palette tab. By default, the
Palette contains a series of tiles, like water, stone, or transitions between these. We use the mouse, or the
arrow keys, to move up or down in the Palette like any tree.")
        Paragraph("To add an item, whether it is a monster, a player, or a floor tile, we press Enter
on it in the Palette. This makes a preview of the item in the scene editor. We can move the preview with the
arrow keys, and press Enter again to place it. A screen reader says "):DoubleQuote():Output("Placed"):DoubleQuote():Text(" and the item's
name, for example "):DoubleQuote():Output("Placed Water"):DoubleQuote():Text(". To leave a preview without placing it, press Escape. With
the mouse, we can drag an item from the Palette onto the scene instead.")
        Paragraph("The example below shows a large grey grid with lines marking the grid spots. In the
bottom left corner of a grey square outline is a small blue square, which in this case is a water tile. The grey
outline shows what the 2D layer's orthographic camera sees, if it is not moved for the game.")
        Image("media/scenes/AddRemoveItems/GridCursor.png", "A grid with one water tile at the bottom left corner of the camera's grey outline, at 0, 0.")
        Paragraph("To describe this in text, the grey outline is 768 by 576 pixels. Each small square
is 32 by 32 pixels, so to find the number of squares in the grid we divide by 32 in each direction: 24 squares
across and 18 up. This also means the water tile takes up 1,024 pixels in the game, and the camera views
442,368 pixels by default. Put another way, each tile is much smaller than the space the camera sees, even
before zooming out.")
        Paragraph("In the same way, we can use the arrow keys to add many tiles. Below is an example
with 12 water tiles in a 2 by 6 block, meaning 2 tiles wide and 6 tall. Running this game would show only the
tiles in the bottom left corner, with the camera pointed at the grey square with the sizes given above. Someone
playing the game would see a blue rectangle in the bottom left, and the rest would be the grey background.")
        Image("media/scenes/AddRemoveItems/WaterTiles.png", "A block of water tiles, 2 wide and 6 tall, starting at 0, 0.")
        Paragraph("Other items can be placed on a tile, such as a character or a monster. Use
Command+1 on Mac, or Control+1 on Windows, to go back to the project window. Focus returns to the Palette tab
where you left it. Then use the arrow keys and Enter, or drag and drop with the mouse. The following shows a
character and a red mouthed monster in the middle of the camera's view.")
        Image("media/scenes/AddRemoveItems/Characters.png", "The water tiles, with a boy and a Red Mouth monster about a quarter of the way into the camera's view.")
    end

    private action Scene3D
        Subsection("Changing the 3D Scene")
        Paragraph("Adding items to a 3D scene is much like adding them to a 2D scene. The main
difference is that 3D graphics use a third coordinate, z, for how far toward or away from us an item is. 3D
items are placed from the same Palette. By default it has a Basic 3D group with primitives such as cubes,
spheres and cylinders, and also lights, sounds and terrain, in addition to 3D shapes related to nature, space,
castles, or roads.")
        Paragraph("While you build, you look through the editor's perspective camera. The mouse can
change how we look at a 3D scene in several ways:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Look around:"):Text(" hold the right mouse button and drag. The camera stays in place
but turns toward where you move the mouse. If you drag toward the right, the camera turns to the right.")
        ListItem():Strong("Orbit:"):Text(" hold the middle mouse button and drag. The camera circles around a
point in the distance, so you keep looking at that point from a new place.")
        ListItem():Strong("Pan:"):Text(" hold Shift and the middle mouse button, and drag.")
        ListItem():Strong("Zoom:"):Text(" use the scroll wheel to move in and out.")

        Paragraph("With the keyboard, we change the camera by selecting it, with Option+F on Mac or Alt+F
on Windows, and then changing its distance, direction, zoom and field of view in the Properties panel. The "):Link("Navigation and Keys", "/experiment/Scenes/navigate.html"):Text(" page has the details.")

        Paragraph("Past the camera, items are added the same way as in 2D: choose one in the Palette,
press Enter, move the preview, and press Enter to place it. The example below shows a cylinder in the middle of
the grid.")
        Image("media/scenes/AddRemoveItems/3DGridCursor.png", "A cylinder on the 3D grid, at 0, 1, 0.")

        Paragraph("While the preview or a selected item is in the 3D editor, the arrow keys move it:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("Left and Right move it along x;")
        ListItem("Up and Down move it forward and backward, along z;")
        ListItem("Command+Up and Command+Down on Mac, or Control+Up and Control+Down on Windows, move it up and down, along y.")
        Paragraph("When you let go of an arrow key, a screen reader says what else is in that spot and
the position, for example "):DoubleQuote():Output("Cube, 3 1 0"):DoubleQuote():Text(". Then press Enter to place the item.")
        Image("media/scenes/AddRemoveItems/CylinderAndCube.png", "A cylinder at 0, 1, 0 and a cube at 3, 1, 0.")
        Image("media/scenes/AddRemoveItems/SphereAndCube.png", "A sphere in the middle of the camera's view, with a cube to its right.")

        Paragraph("A terrain, the ground of a 3D world, is added from Basic 3D in the same way, but it
works a little differently: it always sits at the origin. See "):Link("Terrain in a Scene", "/experiment/Scenes/terrain.html"):Text(".")
    end

    private action Selecting
        Subsection("Selecting Items")
        Paragraph("To go to the scene editor from elsewhere in Quorum Studio, press Command+2 on Mac,
or Control+2 on Windows. Then move the cursor with the arrow keys to the tile or item you want, and press Enter
to select what is there. With the mouse, click the item. You can also select an item from the scene tree.")
        Paragraph("If there are several items in the same spot, press Tab and Shift+Tab to move
between them. A screen reader says each item's name and where it is in the list, for example "):DoubleQuote():Output("Red Mouth, 1 of 2: 0, 0, 0"):DoubleQuote():Text(".")
        Paragraph("When an item is selected, its properties show in the Properties panel, on the
right side of the scene tab. Press Command+P on Mac, or Control+P on Windows, to move into the panel. Press
Escape or Enter there to come back to the scene. The image below shows a red mouthed monster when selected.")
        Image("media/scenes/AddRemoveItems/DeleteMonster.png", "A selected Red Mouth monster at 0, 0, with its properties in the Properties panel on the right.")
        Paragraph("If you have selected an item and decide not to change it, press Escape to leave the
selection.")
    end

    private action Removing
        Subsection("Removing Items")
        Paragraph("Once we have selected the item we want, we press Delete to remove it. On Windows,
Delete or Backspace both work. On Mac, use the Delete key (or Forward Delete).")
        Paragraph("Deleting an item in 3D is the same. You go to the item, press Tab if there is more
than one, and press Delete.")
        Image("media/scenes/AddRemoveItems/Cube.png", "A single cube, a little to the right of the middle of the view.")
        Paragraph("Made a mistake? Adding and deleting can be undone with Quorum Studio's Undo, and
redone with Redo. Changes typed into the Properties panel can't be undone yet, so check them before moving on.")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("In this activity, you and a partner build a small 3D scene together, taking turns, and say
out loud everything you do as you do it. The goal is to get comfortable adding, placing and removing items in
3D, with whichever you prefer of the keyboard or the mouse, and to practice explaining a scene in words. Don't
use terrain yet; you'll build your own ground instead. Partner A starts: open a new 3D scene, add a "):Strong("Cube"):Text(" from Basic 3D, and turn it into a flat floor. Select it, go to the Properties panel, and
set its scale to 10, 0.2 and 10, so it becomes a thin slab 10 meters on each side. Give it a color, such as
green for grass or gray for stone. Then add three or four items on the floor, such as shapes from Basic 3D,
models from one of the kits, and a Point Light. As you work, describe each step to your partner: which item you
chose, how you're moving it (which keys, or what you're doing with the mouse), and where it ends up, using
coordinates, such as "):Quoted("a tree at 2, 0, 3, near the left edge"):Text(".")

        Paragraph("Your partner listens without touching the computer, and sketches the scene from
above on paper, using only what they hear. Then switch. Partner B adds three or four more items while describing
their steps the same way, placing each one in relation to what's already there, such as "):Quoted("a rock 2
meters to the right of the tree"):Text(". Partner B also removes one of Partner A's items, saying which one and
how they found and selected it, and Partner A crosses it off the sketch. When you're done, compare both sketches
with the scene in Quorum Studio. Use Ctrl+Shift+1 on an item to hear its exact position. Where a sketch is
wrong, work out together which part of the description was missing or unclear.")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("Adding and removing items is the start of building any scene. Choosing a spot with
the cursor, selecting what is there, and pressing one key to place or remove it works the same way in 2D and 3D.
Next, we look at all of the keys the scene editor has for moving around, and for moving, turning and sizing
items.")
    end
end
